module dev.slatelang.slate
@tests

import sh.sysl.parsing.*
import sysl.text.Search

// `using` -- the half of the feature a slate program cannot ask about from inside itself.
//
// **`tests/lang/using.sl` is the other half and is the bigger one**: every way out of a block,
// the reverse order, the suppression rule and the refusals a program can catch are all things both
// back ends can be asked, so they are written once and run twice. What is here is what only this
// side can reach -- a refusal that stops the COMPILE rather than the run, a `using` at a file's own
// top level, and the built-in resources answering `dispose`, which needs a socket and a server.

// -- what stops the compile -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **`await using` IS REFUSED, AND THE LIMIT IS THE UNWIND RATHER THAN THE SYNTAX.** Four of the five
// ways out of a block are jumps, where an `await` would be ordinary; the fifth is a fault
// travelling, and its release runs in the middle of an unwind, on a machine that is being taken
// apart. A machine cannot park there -- so waiting on a release would work on four paths and hang on
// the one the feature exists for.
@test
await_using_IS_REFUSED_AND_THE_MESSAGE_NAMES_THE_UNWIND()
    val prog = raw"""
    made(n) = { dispose: () -> print(n) }

    async run() =
        await using a = made("a")

        "ran"

    run()
    """

    val said = fails(prog)

    assert(said.contains("`await using` is not in slate"))
    assert(said.contains("a release has to run while a fault is unwinding"))
end await_using_IS_REFUSED_AND_THE_MESSAGE_NAMES_THE_UNWIND

// **`using` is a SOFT word**, so a program that already calls something `using` is still a program.
@test
using_IS_A_SOFT_WORD_AND_STAYS_AN_ORDINARY_NAME()
    val prog = raw"""
    val using = [1, 2, 3]

    print(using[0], using.length)

    using2(using) = using + 1

    print(using2(41))
    """

    assert_eq(out(prog), "1 3\n42")
end using_IS_A_SOFT_WORD_AND_STAYS_AN_ORDINARY_NAME

// -- a file's own top level -------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **A FILE'S STATEMENTS ARE A BLOCK, so a top-level `using` is released when they end.** That is the
// same rule every other `using` obeys rather than a second one, and it is TypeScript's answer for a
// module. What it does NOT cover is a callback the event loop has yet to run, which `docs/reference/
// statements.md` says in as many words -- a resource a later turn still needs belongs in a function.
@test
A_using_AT_A_FILES_TOP_LEVEL_IS_RELEASED_WHEN_ITS_STATEMENTS_END()
    val prog = raw"""
    made(n) = { dispose: () -> print("dispose " + n) }

    using first = made("first")
    using second = made("second")

    print("the last line")
    """

    assert_eq(out(prog), "the last line\ndispose second\ndispose first")
end A_using_AT_A_FILES_TOP_LEVEL_IS_RELEASED_WHEN_ITS_STATEMENTS_END

// -- what the machine refuses, with a value the checker cannot see ------------------------------------

// **The refusal is at the DECLARATION**, so nothing in the block has run when the reader is told --
// which is the whole reason the check is made where the resource arrives rather than where it is
// released. `anything` is what hands the machine a value whose type nobody wrote down.
@test
A_VALUE_WITH_NO_dispose_IS_REFUSED_BEFORE_THE_BLOCK_RUNS()
    val prog = raw"""
    anything(v) = v

    run() =
        using a = anything(42)

        print("the body ran")

    print(run() catch e -> e.message)
    """

    assert_eq(out(prog), "`using` needs a value with a `dispose` method to call when the block is left, and this is an integer")
end A_VALUE_WITH_NO_dispose_IS_REFUSED_BEFORE_THE_BLOCK_RUNS

// **A `using` name is a `val` and nothing more**, so an assignment to one draws the sentence every
// `val` draws -- and it draws it from the machine, which is where a `val`'s own refusal comes from.
// The release still runs on the way out, the assignment being an ordinary fault travelling.
@test
A_using_NAME_IS_A_val_AND_AN_ASSIGNMENT_TO_ONE_IS_REFUSED()
    val prog = raw"""
    made(n) = { dispose: () -> print("dispose " + n) }

    run() =
        using a = made("a")

        a = 1
        "ran"

    print(run() catch e -> e.message)
    """

    assert_eq(out(prog), "dispose a\n`a` is a `val`, so it is written once")
end A_using_NAME_IS_A_val_AND_AN_ASSIGNMENT_TO_ONE_IS_REFUSED

// -- the built-in resources ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **EVERY BUILT-IN RESOURCE ANSWERS `dispose`, AND FOR A SOCKET IT IS `close` UNDER A SECOND NAME.**
// That is the test each one was put to: where releasing it is already the whole of what its `close`
// does, and that `close` is idempotent and takes nothing but the handle, `dispose` is the same
// native reached by the name `using` looks for.
//
// **The watchdog is what makes this an assertion rather than a hope.** A listener nobody closed keeps
// the loop alive for ever, so a build where a socket did not answer `dispose` fails here by the
// watchdog naming itself -- where without one it would hang this test and every program the suite
// runs after it.
@test
A_LISTENING_SERVER_IS_RELEASED_BY_THE_BLOCK_THAT_HELD_IT()
    val prog = netted(raw"""
    run() =
        using server = listen(0, conn -> null)

        print(localPort(server) > 0)

    run()
    done()
    print("the loop is free")
    """)

    assert_eq(out(prog), "true\nthe loop is free")
end A_LISTENING_SERVER_IS_RELEASED_BY_THE_BLOCK_THAT_HELD_IT

// **A socket answers `dispose` and NOTHING else**, which is `method.sysl`'s line: the rest of what a
// socket does is `slate:net`'s free functions, and a builtin kind answering to a word a program
// might want for its own is the thing that rule exists to stop.
@test
A_SOCKET_ANSWERS_dispose_AND_NOT_THE_REST_OF_slate_net()
    val prog = netted(raw"""
    val server = listen(0, conn -> null)

    print(server.dispose is function)
    print(server.send catch e -> e.message)

    close(server)
    done()
    """)

    assert_eq(out(prog), "true\n`send` is not something a socket can do")
end A_SOCKET_ANSWERS_dispose_AND_NOT_THE_REST_OF_slate_net

// **A database is the object-shaped case of the same rule**: `slate:sqlite` answers a `Db` whose
// `close` is written in slate, so `dispose` is that very function under the name the language looks
// for. Closing twice is not an error there, which is what lets one name stand for both.
@test
A_DATABASE_IS_RELEASED_BY_THE_BLOCK_THAT_OPENED_IT()
    val prog = raw"""
    import { sqlite } from slate:sqlite

    run() =
        using db = sqlite(":memory:")

        db.exec("create table t (a integer)")
        db.run("insert into t values (?)", 7)
        db.query("select a from t")[0].a

    print(run())
    """

    assert_eq(out(prog), "7")
end A_DATABASE_IS_RELEASED_BY_THE_BLOCK_THAT_OPENED_IT
